{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Arise, lift up the lad.</b> After he drinks, and grasp your hand upon him." "<b>And know</b> that according to the order of the sections, Yishmael was already at this time sixteen years old, for when he was born Avraham was eighty-six years old, as it is said: ״ואברם בן שמונים שנה ושש שנים בלדת הגר את ישמעאל״, and at a hundred years Avraham begot Yitzchak, as it is said: ״ואברהם בן מאה שנה בהולד לו את יצחק בנו״, and two years after Yitzchak was weaned — that makes Yishmael sixteen years old in this event. Yet a difficulty arises from this, since Scripture mentions: ״ותן אל הגר שם על שכמה ואת הילד״ — how could she place upon her shoulder a sixteen-year-old? And if you say that ״ואת הילד״ does not refer back to what was said about her shoulder, still we find ״ותשלך את הילד״, and it appears that she had been carrying him in her bosom and now, in the agony of thirst, she cast him from her bosom. Moreover, she called him a child just as Scripture called Yitzchak a child immediately after he was weaned, when he was two years old, as it is said: ״ויגדל הילד ויגמל״. Moreover, the angel of YHWH said to her: ״קומי שאי את הנער״ — and it would have been fitting to say to her, 'Go with the lad'; and furthermore it is written afterward: ״ויהי אלהים את הנער ויגדל״. And what is understood from all this is that he was young in this event. And if the sections are not in their order, we would be compelled to say: there is no earlier and later in the Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:06.156513542Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}